{"records":[{"id":"9xwvvvcsz6","title":"Glucose-induced CRL4COP1-p53 degradation axis amplifies glycometabolism to drive tumorigenesis ","description":"The diabetes-cancer association remains under-explained. Here, we describe a glucose signaling axis that reinforces glucose uptake and glycolysis to consolidate the Warburg effect and overcome tumor suppression. Specifically, glucose-dependent CK2 O-GlcNAcylation impedes its phosphorylation of CSN2, a modification required for the deneddylase CSN to sequester Cullin RING Ligase 4 (CRL4). Glucose therefore elicits CSN-CRL4 dissociation to assemble the CRL4^COP1 E3 ligase, which targets p53 to derepress glycolytic enzymes. Genetic or pharmacologic disruption of the O-GlcNAc-CK2-CSN2-CRL4^COP1 axis abrogates glucose-induced p53 degradation and cancer cell proliferation. Diet-induced overnutrition up-regulates the CRL4^COP1-p53 axis to promote PyMT-induced mammary tumorigenesis in wildtype but not mammary gland-specific p53 knockout mice. These effects of overnutrition are reversed by P28, an investigational peptide inhibitor of COP1-p53 interaction. Thus, glycometabolism self-amplifies via a glucose-induced post-translational modification cascade culminating in CRL4^COP1-mediated p53 degradation. Such mutation-independent p53 checkpoint bypass may represent the carcinogenic origin and targetable vulnerability of hyperglycemia-driven cancer. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:19:12.339Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xwvvvcsz6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xwvvvcsz6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Feng","last_name":"Rao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Su","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yifan","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yjv7ts2s3z","title":"Shootin1a-Mediated Actin–Adhesion Coupling Generates Force to Trigger Structural Plasticity of Dendritic Spines","description":"Dendritic spines constitute the major compartments of excitatory post-synapses. They undergo activity-dependent enlargement, which is thought to increase the synaptic efficacy underlying learning and memory. The activity-dependent spine enlargement requires activation of signaling pathways leading to promotion of actin polymerization within spines. However, the molecular machinery that suffices for this structural plasticity remains unclear. Here we demonstrate that shootin1a links polymerizing actin filaments in spines with the cell adhesion molecules N-cadherin and L1-CAM, thereby mechanically coupling the filaments to the extracellular environment. Synaptic activation enhances shootin1a-mediated actin–adhesion coupling in spines. Promotion of actin polymerization is insufficient for the plasticity: the enhanced actin–adhesion coupling is required for polymerizing actin filaments to push against the membrane for spine enlargement. By integrating cell signaling, cell adhesion, and force generation into the current model of actin-based machinery, we propose a molecular machinery that is sufficient to trigger the activity-dependent spine structural plasticity. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-14T13:29:13.458Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yjv7ts2s3z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yjv7ts2s3z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ria","last_name":"Kastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3199-547X"},{"first_name":"Takunori","last_name":"Minegishi","orcid":"0000-0003-4344-3605"},{"first_name":"Kentarou","last_name":"Baba","orcid":"0000-0002-3002-3129"},{"first_name":"Takeo","last_name":"Saneyoshi","orcid":"0000-0003-2361-3339"},{"first_name":"Hiroko","last_name":"Katsuno-Kambe","orcid":"0000-0003-0292-3506"},{"first_name":"Singh","last_name":"Saranpal","orcid":"0000-0002-6937-8349"},{"first_name":"Yasunori","last_name":"Hayashi","orcid":"0000-0002-7560-3004"},{"first_name":"Naoyuki","last_name":"Inagaki","orcid":"0000-0002-2664-9196"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroscience"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kdfvg2rvjn","title":"Targeting Neddylation Sensitizes Colorectal Cancer to Topoisomerase I Inhibitors by Inactivating the DCAF13-CRL4 Ubiquitin Ligase Complex","description":"the raw data of all replicates conducted for the project \"Targeting Neddylation Sensitizes Colorectal Cancer to Topoisomerase I Inhibitors by Inactivating the DCAF13-CRL4 Ubiquitin Ligase Complex\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-27T14:13:31.651Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kdfvg2rvjn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kdfvg2rvjn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yilun","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Topoisomerase","Colorectal Cancer","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cdgx63gd5y","title":"TRIM21 induces selective autophagy of viruses and bacteria","description":"TRIM21 is an exceptionally versatile ubiquitin ligase that can be directed by antibodies to target oligomeric protein scaffolds, viral capsids, and proteopathic aggregates for intracellular degradation. How the cell degrades these typically resistant substrates remains poorly understood. To address this, we used TRIM21 viral restriction to create a genome-wide phenotypic screen for antibody-dependent capsid degradation. We identify an antimicrobial selective macroautophagy pathway in mammalian cells, which we term \"antibody-directed xenophagy.\" We show this mechanism restricts structurally diverse pathogens, including adenovirus and Salmonella. Using quantitative microscopy, we demonstrate that TRIM21 rapidly intercepts antibody-pathogen complexes, leading to ubiquitin ligase activation. Following this, selective autophagy adaptors are recruited and viral cargoes are delivered to lysosomes. This process reduces Salmonella pathology and bacterial tissue invasion in mice. We propose that TRIM21 evolved through competition with pathogens to induce autophagy of diverse and complex substrates, potentially explaining its versatility for targeted protein degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-29T20:32:28.807Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cdgx63gd5y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cdgx63gd5y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"Rhinesmith","orcid":"0000-0002-9983-0287"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":"U105181010"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029chgv08","name":"Wellcome Trust","grant_id":"223054/Z/21/Z"}],"categories":["Protein Degradation","Autophagy","Host Immune Response","Antiviral Immunity","Bacterial Host Response","Ubiquitin Ligase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f52y9zkpzm","title":"RNF31 inhibition sensitizes tumors to bystander killing by innate and adaptive immune cells","description":"Tumor escape mechanisms for immunotherapy include deficiencies in antigen presentation, diminishing adaptive CD8+ T cell antitumor activity. Although innate NK cells are triggered by loss of MHC class I, their response is often inadequate. To increase tumor susceptibility to both innate and adaptive immune elimination, we performed parallel genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens under NK and CD8+ T cell pressure. We identified all components, RNF31, RBCK1, and SHARPIN, of the linear ubiquitination chain assembly complex (LUBAC). Genetic and pharmacologic ablation of RNF31, an E3 ubiquitin ligase, strongly sensitized melanoma, breast and colorectal cancer cells to both NK and CD8+ T cell killing. This occurred in a TNF-dependent manner, causing loss of A20 and non-canonical IKK complexes from TNF Receptor Complex I. Corroborating this preclinically, a small molecule RNF31 inhibitor sensitized human colon carcinoma organoids to TNF and greatly enhanced immune bystander killing of antigen-loss and antigen presentation machinery-deficient tumor cells. These results merit exploration of RNF31 inhibition as a clinical pharmacological opportunity for immunotherapy-refractory cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-30T07:01:54.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f52y9zkpzm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f52y9zkpzm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengkui","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tvh5nhwx3c","title":"Cullin-RING E3 ubiquitin ligase 4 regulates neurite morphogenesis during neurodevelopment. Tammy et al.","description":"LC-MS/MS raw data for identification of Immunoprecipitated proteins by Cul4a and Cul4b","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-02T14:30:43.614Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tvh5nhwx3c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tvh5nhwx3c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bongki","last_name":"Cho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biomolecule Interaction"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":101725,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":99,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":101725,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":101352,"restricted":51,"embargoed":322},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1632},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":546},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":365},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":321},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":320},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":304},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":300},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":286},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":222},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":193}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":99524},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1646},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":129},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":105},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":59},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":58},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":37},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":24},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":24}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":380},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":289},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":252},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":233},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":201}]}}